    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_8e5e763ab3aa3a70a22e50cc3f8d9fc9f28e418ea02fdd02a199e5877fb37e7c",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The finite weighted sums f_{ε,n}=Σ_{a≤n} μ(a)a^(−ε)ρ_a have two ordered pointwise limits. For fixed x, the correction is spatially finite because its terms vanish for a>1/x, so it stabilizes once n exceeds 1/x and permits n→∞ to define f_ε. Then ε↓0 gives −χ(x) through the stated Möbius identity. These endpoint identifications gain a clear target and limit order, but they do not upgrade pointwise convergence to H-convergence or establish B_nat membership; those remain separate norm-level obligations in the proof framed under RH.",
      "parent_sources": [
